WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.234
In our national lead.

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.369
The writers and actors

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.536
strike that as ground

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.338
Hollywood production to a halt for months

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:05.905
drags on.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.340
The actor union SAG-AFTRA

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.208
joined the Writers

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:09.676
Guild of America strike in July

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.411
and many of the same issues

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:12.712
that drove writers to strike

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.080
are also shared by actors.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.182
The demand for a fair working wage

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.851
and job protections as streaming

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:21.821
has upended the entertainment industry.

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:24.224
And joining me now to discuss is two time

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:25.058
Tony and six

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:26.192
time Emmy Award winning

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.627
actor Bryan Cranston,

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:28.528
a friend of the show.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.829
I think it's fair to say, Bryan,

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.364
thanks for joining us.

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:32.532
Are you encouraged at all

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:33.533
that major studios

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.468
are set to resume talks

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:36.936
with the Writers Guild?

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.304
And where do talks

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:41.174
stand with SAG after the Actors Guild

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:42.242
And do actors

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.377
want to see that that table?

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.079
Oh, we certainly do.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.213
Thanks for having me, Jake.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.114
Good to see you again.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:50.784
The writers have

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:52.352
been out for almost or around

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.587
for months, the actors for two.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:57.690
It's always a good sign

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.159
when you're invited to

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:00.193
to get back

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.828
to the negotiating table

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:04.731
as far as the after SAG end of it.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:08.468
We've been asking the AMPTP to be a part

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.336
of these negotiations

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.938
and to come back to the table.

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:15.842
And thus far, they have refused to accept

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.677
that invitation.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:21.581
We try not to be pessimistic about that.

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.652
Our sister, Union Writers

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:26.486
Guild of America,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:27.921
they are going back

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.390
apparently next week, and it's hopeful.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:31.391
I mean, any time

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.926
you're actually sitting at the table,

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.561
something can happen.

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:35.962
If you're not talking,

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.797
nothing can happen. Yeah.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:38.498
Both the writers

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:39.299
and the actors unions

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:40.900
have visually been united,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:41.568
as demonstrated

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:42.535
by a large joint

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.071
rally in Los Angeles Wednesday.

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:47.340
Do you think that unity is going to hold

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.275
if if one side reaches an

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.677
agreement before the other?

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.179
I hope it does.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:56.616
We we are so related in our storytelling

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.421
capacity that it it does behoove us

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.158
and helps each others to stay united.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.594
I hope that we do.

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:10.530
We have some slight different issues

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.966
than the Writers Guild have,

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:15.568
but for the most part,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.438
I would say 75, 80% of

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:21.574
their issues are also our issues as well.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:22.742
A lot of that has to do

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.811
with how streaming has upended

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:27.180
the business model on which

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.082
so many careers

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:31.484
and lifestyles were were built.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.920
The industry was built.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.221
Let me play this clip.

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.723
This hopefully can help us

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.658
and our audience understand some of this.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:39.392
This is from your friend

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.528
and fellow Breaking Bad costar, actor

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:42.495
Aaron Ball.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:44.764
Aaron Paul, take a listen.

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:49.502
The show's live, live forever.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:50.870
All these streamers.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:54.374
It goes through waves, you know.

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:54.674
And I mean,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.942
I just saw just the other day

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.945
the Breaking Bad was trending on Netflix.

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:01.080
And it's just it's such common sense.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.849
And I think a lot of these streamers,

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.918
they know that they have been

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:08.221
getting away with not paying people

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:10.023
just fair wage

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.225
and now it's time to pony up.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:14.294
So it might be tough

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:15.361
for people to understand

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.398
breaking Bad is airing on Netflix.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.532
It was originally on AMC

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.967
and now it's on Netflix.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.603
And actors aren't getting paid for it.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:27.006
Well, they're if you literally,

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:30.210
figuratively, you are getting paid.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:34.214
But the scale is so far below

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:35.915
what we're used to

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.150
There used to be several

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.551
different revenue streams

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.253
for a working actor

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.487
to cobble together

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:42.722
to actually make a living.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.057
And you needed all of them

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.925
to be able to do that.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.161
Well, those have been whisked away.

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:52.765
And what was called New Media years ago

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.667
now, the streaming market,

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.302
we know what it is.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.339
And we know that all the legacy studios

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:02.275
have switched their entire business model

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:05.278
to become part of that contest.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:08.481
To be a leader in the streaming wars.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:11.551
And it has created a situation

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:14.153
where the AMPTP is taking

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:18.825
an old and out modeled business model and

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:20.393
coming up

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:21.060
with their

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.296
contract offers, according to that.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:25.398
Well, everything is changed

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:29.135
This business has changed demonstrably.

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:30.970
So we need to look at

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.106
what are the new situations

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:33.940
that we're dealing with,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.608
what are the new rules

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.043
that we're playing by

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:40.513
and come up with new agreements that that

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:42.181
answer to that.

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.950
There's also the simple fact

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.451
that about five weeks ago,

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:49.222
The New York Times did an exposé on the

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.758
the rents in Los Angeles

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:54.360
from 2000 to two to 23

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:58.531
and they showed that rents in Los

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:02.201
Angeles went up 35% on average.

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:04.737
At the same time,

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:09.242
the increase in pay went up only 6%.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.110
So it doesn't take a mathematician

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:11.878
to realize

00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:12.679
that inflation

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:14.714
has had a tremendous effect

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:16.549
on the working actor.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:16.849
Yeah.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:17.717
And I know one of the other

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:18.751
big sticking points

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.520
is, for instance, on Netflix,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.120
you don't even know

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.689
how many people are watching

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:25.258
because Netflix does not share the data.

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:26.859
That is one of the big sticking points

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:27.860
for the writers.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.962
And the actors let you know

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.164
because the ratings

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.098
used to be the way

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:34.801
that the networks would let people know

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:36.169
that was all public

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.737
and that that information

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:39.372
is now being guarded.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.107
That's true in an unbelievable way.

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:42.508
Bryan Cranston, always good to see you.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:43.776
Thank you so much.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:46.145
We all hope that this strike end soon

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.514
and that living wages are paid.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:48.915
Thank you.

